<task id="032" project="spaarkeai-compose-r2">
  <metadata>
    <title>FR-18 serial action queueing in ConversationPane</title>
    <phase>3 Inline Editing UX</phase>
    <gate>green</gate>
    <status>completed</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <model-tier>sonnet</model-tier>
    <effort>high</effort>
    <parallel-group>phase3-ui</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>true</parallel-safe>
    <deps>none</deps>
    <tags>frontend,react,testing</tags>
    <estimated-effort>high</estimated-effort>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>
    Make rapidly-invoked Compose AI actions run one at a time in dispatch order in the ConversationPane. When the user
    fires Compare then Draft in quick succession, each must stream to completion before the next starts — producing two
    ordered, non-interleaved streams and ordered ledger writes (owner decision: serial queue, not parallel). This is a
    predictability guarantee for ledger ordering (design §2.3/§7, spec FR-18).
  </prompt>

  <role>
    Frontend developer expert in React async state, streaming (SSE) consumption, and queue/serialization patterns.
  </role>

  <goal>
    A serial action queue in ConversationPane that accepts inbound `compose_action_request` dispatches, enqueues them in
    arrival order, and executes them strictly one-at-a-time (each streams to completion before the next dispatch begins),
    yielding ordered non-interleaved streams + ordered ledger writes. RTL/behavioral tests prove ordering under rapid-fire.
  </goal>

  <context>
    <background>
      ConversationPane is a thin host (≤300 lines) delegating behavior to focused sibling hooks (useInjectionQueue,
      useEventBatch, useCommandRouting, etc.). Add the serial queue as a new focused hook (`useSerialActionQueue.ts`) rather
      than inflating the host. It consumes `compose_action_request` events (from FR-14 / task 030's toolbar) and drives them
      through the existing `dispatchConsumer` seam serially. This task is parallel-safe with 030/031 (different files:
      ConversationPane vs ComposeEditor).
    </background>
    <relevant-files>
      <file change="create">src/solutions/SpaarkeAi/src/components/conversation/useSerialActionQueue.ts</file>
      <file change="modify">src/solutions/SpaarkeAi/src/components/conversation/ConversationPane.tsx (wire the serial queue hook)</file>
      <file change="reference">src/solutions/SpaarkeAi/src/components/conversation/useInjectionQueue.ts (existing queue hook pattern to mirror)</file>
      <file change="reference">src/solutions/SpaarkeAi/src/components/conversation/useEventBatch.ts (existing PaneEventBus batch consumption)</file>
    </relevant-files>
    <dependencies>
      <dependency task="030" status="pending">FR-14 toolbar — emits the `compose_action_request` events this queue serializes (contract dependency, not file dependency).</dependency>
    </dependencies>
  </context>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="ADR-030">Consume the typed `compose_action_request` discriminant from PaneEventBus; no `any` payloads.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-040">Serial execution guarantees ordered ledger writes (storage precedes rendering); the queue must NOT let a later action's ledger write land before an earlier one's.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Dispatch through the shipped session-dispatch seam (`dispatchConsumer` → `/dispatch`); no new endpoint, no client-side routing (ADR-039).</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">ConversationPane stays a thin host — behavior lives in the new focused hook, not the host component (matches the task-045 decomposition rule in the file header).</constraint>
  </constraints>

  <knowledge>
    <topic>React serial async queue; SSE stream completion gating; PaneEventBus consumption; ordered ledger writes</topic>
    <files>
      <file>.claude/adr/ADR-030-pane-event-bus.md</file>
      <file>.claude/adr/ADR-040-session-ledger.md</file>
      <file>projects/spaarkeai-compose-r2/design.md</file>
      <file>projects/spaarkeai-compose-r2/spec.md</file>
    </files>
    <patterns>
      <pattern name="Serial action queue" location="spec.md FR-18 / design.md §2.3">Rapidly-invoked actions run one at a time in dispatch order; each streams to completion before the next starts.</pattern>
      <pattern name="Focused queue hook" location="src/solutions/SpaarkeAi/src/components/conversation/useInjectionQueue.ts">Existing per-concern hook pattern the serial queue should mirror.</pattern>
    </patterns>
  </knowledge>

  <justification>
    NEW hook `useSerialActionQueue.ts`. (1) Existing — `useInjectionQueue` manages context-injection ordering, not
    dispatch serialization of streaming AI actions; `useEventBatch` batches events but does not gate on stream completion.
    (2) Extension — the serialization concern (await stream-N complete before starting stream-N+1) is distinct; folding it
    into an existing hook would conflate batching with execution-gating. (3) Cost-of-doing-nothing — without it, firing
    Compare then Draft interleaves two SSE streams and produces out-of-order ledger writes, breaking FR-18's ordering bar.
  </justification>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0">Context check. Load ADR-030/040 + design §2.3 + the existing conversation hooks.</step>
    <step order="1">Implement `useSerialActionQueue`: a FIFO queue of `compose_action_request` items; a single in-flight worker that awaits the current dispatch's stream-completion (and its ledger write) before dequeuing the next.</step>
    <step order="2">Drive each item through the existing `dispatchConsumer` seam; expose queue state (pending count, in-flight id) for UI affordance if needed.</step>
    <step order="3">Handle edge cases: an errored/aborted action must still release the queue (no deadlock); a duplicate rapid dispatch is enqueued in order.</step>
    <step order="4">Wire the hook into ConversationPane (thin — just consume the event source + mount the hook).</step>
    <step order="5">Write behavioral tests proving ordering under rapid-fire; `npm run build`; report.</step>
  </steps>

  <ui-tests>
    <ui-test name="serial ordering under rapid-fire">Firing Compare then Draft in quick succession produces two streams that do NOT interleave — the second dispatch does not begin until the first stream completes; assert dispatch/stream order.</ui-test>
    <ui-test name="ordered ledger writes">The ledger-write side effects occur in dispatch order (earlier action's write precedes the later action's) — assert the recorded write order matches enqueue order.</ui-test>
    <ui-test name="error releases the queue">An action that errors/aborts mid-stream still allows the next queued action to run (NEGATIVE: no deadlock on failure).</ui-test>
  </ui-tests>

  <outputs>
    <output type="code">src/solutions/SpaarkeAi/src/components/conversation/useSerialActionQueue.ts</output>
    <output type="code">src/solutions/SpaarkeAi/src/components/conversation/ConversationPane.tsx (wire hook)</output>
    <output type="test">useSerialActionQueue behavioral tests (co-located)</output>
    <output type="report">Task completion report incl. bundle impact</output>
  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria testable="true">
    <criterion testable="true">Firing two actions (e.g., Compare then Draft) in quick succession produces two ordered, non-interleaved streams — the second does not start until the first completes.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Ledger writes occur in dispatch order; a later action's write never precedes an earlier action's (ADR-040 ordering).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">An errored or aborted in-flight action releases the queue so subsequent actions still run (NEGATIVE: no deadlock).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The queue consumes the typed `compose_action_request` discriminant and dispatches only through the shipped `dispatchConsumer` seam (no new endpoint/routing).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">ConversationPane remains a thin host (logic in `useSerialActionQueue`); `npm run build` green.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <execution>
    <skill>.claude/skills/task-execute/SKILL.md</skill>
  </execution>

